ak-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Good question! If new devices are added=
 to the user's ThinQ account after the initial <strong>GET /devices</strong=
> call, your service won't automatically know about them unless you call th=
e <strong>GET /devices</strong> method again to retrieve an updated list of=
 devices.</p><h3>Here's how to handle this:</h3><ol><li><p><strong>Initial =
call</strong>: You call <strong>GET /devices</strong> once to get the list =
of devices at the start. This gives you all the devices registered at that =
moment.</p></li><li><p><strong>Device change detection</strong>: If new dev=
ices are added or removed after this initial call, your service won't autom=
atically know about those changes. To account for this:</p><ul><li><strong>=
Option 1</strong>: Periodically call <strong>GET /devices</strong> again at=
 intervals (e.g., daily, weekly, etc.) to ensure you're working with the la=
test list of devices. This way, you can detect any new devices that were ad=
ded since the last time you called the method.</li><li><strong>Option 2</st=
rong>: If the API supports <strong>webhooks</strong> or notifications for d=
evice changes (some APIs provide this), you could set up a webhook that lis=
tens for updates (like new devices being added). If ThinQ offers such funct=
ionality, you would receive notifications when new devices are added, and y=
ou wouldn't need to poll for device changes manually.</li></ul></li></ol><p=
>So, in summary: <strong>GET /devices</strong> should be called again if yo=
u need to refresh the device list to account for any changes over time. The=
 frequency of how often you call it depends on how frequently devices are l=
ikely to change or how up-to-date your device data needs to be.</p><p>Let m=
e know if you'd like further clarification or if you'd like to explore webh=
ooks if ThinQ offers them!</p></div></div></div></div><div class=3D"mb-2 fl=
